1) Insure you can run uv. You might need to do something like: >sudo pip3 install uv. This is not recommended:
   a better solution is to go to the documentation for uv and install it properly.
2) Run: >uv pip install -r requirements.txt. Hopefully this works on your machine.
3) Run: >source .venv/bin/activate. You might also need to run: >uv install PyQt6. This is required because
   anal_functs.py currently includes a plot routine that uses pyqtgraph. This is not currently
   being used and we really should delete it, but pyqtgraph is a reasonbabl alternative to
   matplotlib, and the included routine will save time if you want to try it out.
4) anal_functs.py is an early effort to supply a general purpose plotting script. It is
   intended to be useful for plotting spectrums of complexoids, impulse responses of filters
   (both real and complex), also to just plot two waveforms (possibly complex) for comparison
   during debut (for example comparing a python simulation output to swift simulation output.
   Examples:
   a) to view the spectral response of a signal having a period of 16 samples:
      > python3 anal_functs.py Osc4.dat -p 16 --lims -20 110
   b) to view the impulse responses from a number of filters:
      > python3 anal_functs.py CmplxFltr5a.dat CmplxFltr5b.dat CmplxFltr5c.dat CmplxFltr5d.dat --lims -40 35
   c) to compare two data waveforms (exactly two files must be specified - if complex, each file has two columns)
      > python3 anal_functs.py -d CmplxFltr5c.dat Fltr5c.dat 
   d) to plot multiple filter responses, just specify multiple data files after specifying --lims options